---
// REVIEW: Layer 2 interpretation for the five-part questionnaire (5PQ),
// drafted for Craig's review. Static, always visible, rendered at build time.
// FAQ answers come from five-part-questionnaire.content.ts and are shown word
// for word (they also feed the page's FAQPage JSON-LD).
import { ACCURACY, FAQ, SOURCES } from '../../tools/five-part-questionnaire.content';
import '../../styles/interpretation.css';

const s = SOURCES;
---

<!-- REVIEW -->
<section class="interpretation" aria-labelledby="understanding-5pq">
	<h2 id="understanding-5pq">Understanding your 5PQ score</h2>

	<h3 id="what-it-measures">What the score measures</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.measures.a}</p>
	<p>
		The score is the number of "yes" answers, with five questions chosen from a longer set
		(<a href={s.hakim.href} rel="noopener">{s.hakim.label}</a>).
	</p>

	<h3 id="score-bands">Score bands</h3>
	<p>
		There are two bands: 0 or 1 "yes" is a negative screen, and 2 to 5 is positive. A higher count has no separate
		meaning.
	</p>
	<p>{FAQ.accuracy.a}</p>
	<table class="interp-table">
		<caption>How often two or more "yes" answers agreed with a hypermobility assessment</caption>
		<thead>
			<tr><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">Sensitivity</th><th scope="col">Specificity</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{ACCURACY.map(r => (
				<tr><td>{r.group}</td><td>{r.sensitivity}</td><td>{r.specificity}</td><td><a href={s[r.study].href} rel="noopener">{s[r.study].label}</a></td></tr>
			))}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		So a negative screen is fairly reassuring, while a positive one is a prompt for a check, not a finding.
	</p>
	<p>{FAQ.heds.a}</p>

	<h3 id="change">What a change in score means</h3>
	<p>
		No minimal important change exists. Most questions are about your history, so answers should rarely change, and a
		different result usually means a question was read differently. Swedish students who answered twice gave highly
		repeatable totals (<a href={s.glans.href} rel="noopener">{s.glans.label}</a>).
	</p>

	<h3 id="what-it-does-not-measure">What it does not measure</h3>
	<p>{FAQ.diagnosis.a}</p>
	<p>
		It does not measure pain, how much hypermobility affects you, or your current joint range. The questionnaire has not
		been validated in children (<a href={s.malfait.href} rel="noopener">{s.malfait.label}</a>). For today's range, use the
		<a href="/tools/beighton-score/">Beighton score</a>.
	</p>

	<h3 id="taking-it-to-your-gp">Taking this to your GP or consultant</h3>
	<p>
		Bring the count, the date, which questions you answered "yes" and your Beighton score if you have one. Describe the
		5PQ as a published five-question screen for hypermobility, including past hypermobility. It can support a request
		for a hypermobility examination; it cannot diagnose anything. The <a href="/tools/heds-criteria/">hEDS criteria
		checklist</a> applies both scores together. See also
		<a href="/testing/preparing-for-gp-appointment/">preparing for a GP appointment</a>.
	</p>

	<div class="tool-foot">
		<slot name="disclaimer" />
		<details class="sources" id="sources">
			<summary>Sources and licence</summary>
			<slot name="citation" />
			<ul class="interp-sources">
				<li>Hakim AJ, Grahame R. A simple questionnaire to detect hypermobility: an adjunct to the assessment of patients with diffuse musculoskeletal pain. <em>Int J Clin Pract</em> 2003;57(3):163–166. <a href={s.hakim.href} rel="noopener">PubMed</a></li>
				<li>Glans M, Humble MB, Elwin M, Bejerot S. Self-rated joint hypermobility: the five-part questionnaire evaluated in a Swedish non-clinical adult population. <em>BMC Musculoskelet Disord</em> 2020;21:174. <a href={s.glans.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Schlager A, Ahlqvist K, Pingel R, et al. Validity of the self-reported five-part questionnaire as an assessment of generalized joint hypermobility in early pregnancy. <em>BMC Musculoskelet Disord</em> 2020;21:514. <a href={s.schlager.href} rel="noopener">Full text</a></li>
				<li>Malfait F, Francomano C, Byers P, et al. The 2017 international classification of the Ehlers-Danlos syndromes. <em>Am J Med Genet C Semin Med Genet</em> 2017;175(1):8–26. <a href={s.malfait.href} rel="noopener">DOI</a></li>
			</ul>
		</details>
	</div>
</section>
